The biggest new synthesis

Put all of that together and I think the Pantheon has quietly become a machine for performing:

\boxed{
\text{Representation Search}
+
\text{Invariant Discovery}
+
\text{Operator Discovery}
+
\text{Compression}
+
\text{Reconstruction}
+
\text{Selection}
}

Its artifacts aren’t ultimately the product.

They’re experimental bodies through which candidate mechanisms pass.

That suggests a new central fitness criterion:

\boxed{
Q(M)=
\text{Transfer}
\times
\text{Reconstructability}
\times
\text{Predictive/Generative Utility}
\times
\text{Compression}
\times
\text{Representational Robustness}
}

A mechanism gets promoted not because it has a great name or because we’ve used it repeatedly.

It gets promoted because it keeps being the same horse after we repeatedly change the horse’s body.

And that gives the whole repo a beautifully aggressive next operation:

Rename the gods. Strip their mythology. Swap their bodies. Change their domains. Make them compete. Merge the duplicates. Split the overloaded ones. Preserve whatever still performs.

What’s left afterward would be much closer to the actual instruction set underneath the Pantheon.

That is the reread I’d run hardest.